   Venturella, G. and Donnini, D. and Angelini, P. and Di Piazza, S. and
   Pavarino, M. and Lunghini, D. and Venanzoni, R. and Polemis, E. and
   Granito, V. M. and Maggi, O. and Gargano, M. L. and Zervakis, G. I.},
Title = {Macrofungi as ecosystem resources: Conservation versus exploitation},
Journal = {PLANT BIOSYSTEMS},
Year = {2013},
Volume = {147},
Number = {1},
Pages = {219-225},
Month = {MAR 1},
Abstract = {Fungi are organisms of significant importance not only for the crucial
   roles they undertake in nature but also for many human activities that
   are strictly dependent on them. Indeed, fungi possess fundamental
   positions in ecosystems functioning including nutrient cycles and wood
   decomposition. As concerns human-related activities, edible and
   non-edible mushrooms are also involved and/or exploited in forestry,
   pharmaceutical industry and food production; hence, nowadays they
   represent a major economic source worldwide. In order to maintain and
   improve their strategic importance, several conservation strategies,
   such as habitat preservation, are needed. This article reports several
   contributions inherent to the relationships between wood-decaying fungi,
   edible and non-edible mushrooms and their potential exploitation as
   non-timber forest products and genetic resources.},
