Title = {Nanodiamond-Infused Microgels for pH-Tunable Rheology, Lubrication, and
   UV Protection},
Journal = {ADVANCED FUNCTIONAL MATERIALS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {35},
Number = {46},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {Ultraviolet (UV) radiation exposure poses a potent threat to human
   health, with skin cancer cases still prevalent. While skin care
   formulations mitigate the situation, inherent problematic issues,
   including toxicity and unpredictable rheology, exist in many of these
   systems. In this study, the functional properties of carboxylated
   nanodiamond (cND) infused polyacrylic acid (PAA) hydrogels are explored
   as a potential alternative in terms of their UV protection, rheology
   control, and lubrication (skin-feel) properties. cNDs, carbon-based
   nanoparticles (approximate to 200 nm), are used because of their innate
   biocompatible nature, UV filter capacity and tunable surface functional
   groups allowing for modulating interaction with the PAA matrix. In
   particular, it is demonstrated these properties can be controlled
   through varying pH and cND concentration. Dynamic rheology and confocal
   fluorescence microscopy reveal different degrees of microstructural
   reinforcement enabled by changing the nature of polymer-cND and
   interparticle interactions above (pH 5.5) and below the pKa (pH 4.1) of
   carboxyl groups. Tribological experiments in contrast, suggest that
   lubrication properties to be primarily governed by microgel particles at
   either pH. Most importantly, UV absorption and simulated irradiation
   studies show these systems to be photostable with broad-spectrum UV
   attenuation capacity rivalling that of commercial systems, while
   preserving gel microstructure after prolonged UV irradiation.},
